C Providence, for the most part, set us upon a _level_, and
  observes a kind of proportion in its dispensations towards us.
  @Addison’s Spectator, {Nº. 255.}
C I suppose, by the stile of old friend, and the like, it must
  be somebody there of his own _level_; among whom his party
  have, indeed, more friends than I could wish. @Swift.
S 4. An instrument whereby masons adjust their work.
C The _level_ is from two to ten feet long, that it may reach
  over a considerable length of the work: if the plumb-line
  hang just upon the perpendicular, when the _level_ is set flat
  down upon the work, the work is level; but if it hangs on
  either side the perpendicular, the floor or work must be raised
  on that side, till the plumb-line hang exactly on the perpen-
  dicular. @Moxon’s Mechanical Exercises.
S 5. Rule: borrowed from the mechanick level.
V Be the fair _level_ of thy actions laid,
  As temp’rance wills, and prudence may persuade,
  And try if life be worth the liver’s care. @Prior.
S 6. The line of direction in which any missive weapon is aimed.
V I stood i’ th’ _level_
  Of a full charg’d confederacy, and gave thanks
  To you that choked it. @Shakespeare’s Henry {VIII.}
V As if that name,
  Shot from the deadly _level_ of a gun,
  Did murther her. @Shakes. Romeo and Juliet.
V Thrice happy is that humble pair,
  Beneath the _level_ of all care,
  Over whose heads those arrows fly,
  Of sad distrust and jealousy. @Waller.
S 7. The line in which the sight passes.
V Fir’d at first sight with what the muse imparts,
  In fearless youth we tempt the heights of arts;
  While from the bounded _level_ of our mind
  Short views we take, nor see the lengths behind. @Pope.
E {Le´veller.} _n. s._ [from _level._]
S 1. One who makes any thing even.
S 2. One who destroys superiority; one who endeavours to bring
  all to the same state of equality.
C You are an everlasting _leveller_; you won’t allow encourage-
  ment to extraordinary merit. @Collier on Pride.
E {Le´velness.} _n. s._ [from _level._]
S 1. Evenness; equality of surface.
S 2. Equality with something else.
C The river Tiber is expressed lying along, for so you must
  remember to draw rivers, to express their _levelness_ with the
  earth. @Peacham.
E {Le´ven.} _n. s._ [_levain_, French.]
S 1. Ferment; that which being mixed in bread makes it rise
  and ferment.
S 2. Any thing capable of changing the nature of a greater mass;
  any thing that tinctures the whole.
C The matter fermenteth upon the old _leven_, and becometh
  more acrid. @Wiseman’s Surgery.
C As to the pestilential _levains_ conveyed in goods, it is a safe
  opinion. @Arbuthnot on Air.
E {Le´ver.} _n. s._ [_levier_, French.]
C The second mechanical power, is a balance supported by
  a hypomochlion; only the centre is not in the middle, as
  in the common balance, but near one end; for which rea-
  son it is used to elevate or raise a great weight; whence
  comes the name _lever_. @Harris.
C Have you any _leavers_ to lift me up again, being down.
  @Shakespeare’s Henry {IV.}
V Some draw with cords, and some the monster drive
  With rolls and _levers_. @Denham.
C In a _leaver_, the motion can be continued only for so short
  a space, as may be answerable to that little distance betwixt
  the fulciment and the weight; which is always by so much
  lesser, as the disproportion betwixt the weight and the power
  is greater, and the motion itself more easy. @Wilkins’s Magick.
V Some hoisting _leavers_, some the wheels prepare. @Dryd.
C You may have a wooden _lever_, forked at the ends. @Mort.
E {Le´veret.} [_leivre_, French.] A young hare.
V Their travels o’er that silver field does show,
  Like track of _leverets_ in morning snow. @Waller.
E {Leve´t.} _n. s._ [from _lever_, French.] A blast on the trumpet;
  probably that by which the soldiers are called in the morning.
V He that led the cavalcade,
  Wore a sowgelder’s flagellet,
  On which he blew as strong a _levet_;
  As well-fee’d lawyer on his breviate. @Hudibras.
E {Leverook.} _n. s._ [laƿere, Saxon.] This word is retained in
  Scotland, and denotes the lark.
C The smaller birds have their particular seasons; as, the
  _leverook_. @Walton’s Angler.
C If the lufft faa ’twill smoore aw the _leverooks_. @Scotch Prov.
E {Le´viable.} _adj._ [from _levy_.] That may be levied.
C The sums which any agreed to pay, and were not brought
  in, were to be _leviable_ by course of law. @Bacon’s Henry {VII.}
E {_LEVI´ATHAN._} _n. s._ [לויתן.] A water animal mentioned in
  the book of _Job_. By some imagined the crocodile, but in
  poetry generally taken for the whale.
@column
V We may, as bootless, spend our vain command
  Upon th’ enraged soldiers in their spoil,
  As send our precepts to th’ _leviathan_,
  To come ashore. @Shakespeare’s Henry {V.}
C Canst thou draw out _leviathan_ with an hook? @Job.
V More to embroil the deep; _leviathan_,
  And his unwieldy train, in dreadful sport
  Tempest the loosen’d brine. @Thomson’s Winter.
E To {LE´VIGATE.} _v. a._ [_lævigo_, Latin.]
S 1. To rub or grind to an impalpable powder.
S 2. To mix till the liquor becomes smooth and uniform.
C The chyle is white, as consisting of salt, oil, and water,
  much _levigated_ or smooth. @Arbuthnot on Aliment.
E {Leviga´tion.} _n. s._ [from _levigate_.]
C _Levigation_ is the reducing of hard bodies, as coral, tutty,
  and precious stones, into a subtile powder, by grinding upon
  marble with a muller; but unless the instruments are ex-
  tremely hard, they will so wear as to double the weight of
  the medicine. @Quincy.
E {Le´vite.} _n. s._ [_levita_, Latin, from _Levi_.]
S 1. One of the tribe of Levi; one born to the office of priest-
  hood among the Jews.
C In the Christian church, the office of deacons succeeded in
  the place of the _levites_ among the Jews, who were as mi-
  nisters and servants to the priests. @Ayliffe’s Parergon.
S 2. A priest: used in contempt.
E {Levi´tical.} _adj._ [from _levite_.] Belonging to the levites;
  making part of the religion of the Jews.
C By the _levitical_ law, both the man and the woman were
  stoned to death; so heinous a crime was the sin of adultery.
  @Ayliffe’s Parergon.
E {Le´vity.} _n. s._ [_levitas_, Latin.]
S 1. Lightness; not heaviness: the quality by which any body
  has less weight than another.
C He gave the form of _levity_ to that which ascended; to that
  which descended, the form of gravity. @Raleigh.
C This bubble, by reason of its comparative _levity_ to the
  fluidity that encloses it, would necessarily ascend to the top.
  @Bentley’s Sermons.
S 2. Inconstancy; changeableness.
C They every day broached some new thing; which restless
  _levity_ they did interpret to be their growing in spiritual per-
  fection. @Hooker.
V Where wigs with wigs, with sword-knots sword-knots
  strive,
  Beaus banish beaus, and coaches coaches drive,
  This erring mortals _levity_ may call. @Pope.
S 3. Unsteadiness; laxity of mind.
V I unbosom’d all my secrets to thee;
  Not out of _levity_, but over-pow’r’d
  By thy request. @Milton’s Agonistes.
S 4. Idle pleasure; vanity.
C He never employed his omnipotence out of _levity_ or osten-
  tation, but as the necessities of men required. @Calamy.
S 5. Trifling gaiety; want of seriousness.
V Our graver business frowns at this _levity_. @Shakespeare.
C Hopton abhorred the licence, and the _levities_, with which
  he saw too many corrupted. @Clarendon, b. {viii.}
C That spirit of religion and seriousness vanished, and a
  spirit of _levity_ and libertinism, infidelity and prophaneness,
  started up in the room of it. @Atterbury’s Sermons.
E To {LE´VY.} _v. a._ [_lever_, French.]
S 1. To raise; to bring together men.
C He resolved to finish the conquest of Ireland, and to that
  end _levied_ a mighty army. @Davies on Ireland.
S 2. To raise money.
C _Levy_ a tribute unto the Lord of the men of war. @Numb.
C Instead of a ship, he should _levy_ upon his county such a
  sum of money. @Clarendon.
S 3. To make war. This sense, though _Milton_’s, seems im-
  proper.
V They live in hatred, enmity, and strife,
  Among themselves, and _levy_ cruel wars. @Milton.
E {Le´vy.} _n. s._ [from the verb.]
S 1. The act of raising money or men.
C They have already contributed all their superfluous hands,
  and every new _levy_ they make must be at the expence of
  their farms and commerce. @Addison’s State of the War.
S 2. War raised.
V Treason has done his worst: nor steel, nor poison,
  Malice domestick, foreign _levy_, nothing
  Can touch him further! @Shakespeare’s Macbeth.
E {LEWD.} _adj._ [læƿede, Saxon.]
S 1. Lay; not clerical. Obsolete.
C For _lewyd_ men this book I writ. @Bishop Grosthead.
V So these great clerks their little wisdom shew
  To mock the _lewd_, as learn’d in this as they. @Davies.
S 2. Wicked; bad; naughty.
C If some be admitted into the ministry, either void of
  learning, or _lewd_ in life, are all the rest to be con-
  demned? @Whitgift.
