C Before they did oppress the people, only by colour of a
  _lewd_ custom, they did afterwards use the same oppressions by
  warrant. @Davies on Ireland.
S 3. Lustful; libidinous.
V He is not lolling on a _lewd_ love bed,
  But on his knees at meditation. @Shakespeare’s Rich. {III.}
V Then _lewd_ Anchemolus he laid in dust,
  Who stain’d his stepdam’s bed with impious lust. @Dryden.
E {Le´wdly.} _adj._ [from _lewd_.]
S 1. Wickedly; naughtily.
V A sort of naughty persons, _lewdly_ bent,
  Have practis’d dangerously against your state. @Shakesp.
S 2. Libidinously; lustfully.
V He lov’d fair lady Eltred, _lewdly_ lov’d,
  Whose wanton pleasures him too much did please,
  That quite his heart from Guendeline remov’d. @Spenser.
V So _lewdly_ dull his idle works appear,
  The wretched texts deserve no comments here. @Dryden.
E {Le´wdness.} _n. s._ [from _lewd_.] Lustful licentiousness.
V Suffer no _lewdness_, nor indecent speech,
  Th’ apartment of the tender youth to reach. @Dryd. Juv.
C Damianus’s letter to Nicholas is an authentick record of
  the _lewdnesses_ committed under the reign of celibacy. @Atterbury.
E {Le´wdster.} _n. s._ [from _lewd_.] A lecher; one given to cri-
  minal pleasures.
V Against such _lewdsters_, and their lechery,
  Those that betray them do no treachery. @Shakespeare.
E {_LE´WIS D’OR._} _n. s._ [French.] A golden French coin, in
  value twelve livres, now settled at seventeen shillings. @Dict.
E {Lexico´grapher.} _n. s._ [λεξικὸν and γράφω; _lexicographe_,
  French.] A writer of dictionaries; a harmless drudge, that
  busies himself in tracing the original, and detailing the signi-
  fication of words.
C Commentators and _lexicographers_ acquainted with the Sy-
  riac language, have given these hints in their writings on
  scripture. @Watts’s Improvement of the Mind.
E {Lexico´graphy.} _n. s._ [λεξικὸν and γράφω.] The art or prac-
  tice of writing dictionaries.
E {LE´XICON.} _n. s._ [λεξικὸν.] A dictionary; a book teaching
  the signification of words.
C Though a linguist should pride himself to have all the
  tongues that Babel cleft the world into, yet if he had not
  studied the solid things in them as well as the words and _lexi-
  cons_, yet he were nothing so much to be esteemed a learned
  man as any yeoman competently wise in his mother dialect
  only. @Milton.
E {Ley.} _n. s._
C _Ley_, _lee_, _lay_, are all from the Saxon leaȝ, a field or pas-
  ture, by the usual melting of the letter ȝ or g. @Gibson’s Cam.
E {Li´able.} _n. s._ [_liable_, from _lier_, old French.] Obnoxious;
  not exempt; subject.
V But what is strength without a double share
  Of wisdom? vast, unwieldy, burthensome,
  Proudly secure, yet _liable_ to fall
  By weakest subtleties. @Milton’s Agonistes.
C The English boast of Spenser and Milton, who neither of
  them wanted genius or learning; and yet both of them are
  _liable_ to many censures. @Dryden’s Juvenal.
C This, or any other scheme, coming from a private hand,
  might be _liable_ to many defects. @Swift.
E {Li´ar.} _n. s._ [from _lie_. This word would analogically be _lier_;
  but this orthography has prevailed, and the convenience of
  distinction from _lier_, he who lies down, is sufficient to con-
  firm it.] One who tells falshood; one who wants veracity.
V She’s like a _liar_, gone to burning hell!
  ’Twas I that kill’d her. @Shakespeare’s Othello.
V He approves the common _liar_, fame,
  Who speaks him thus at Rome. @Shakesp. Ant. and Cleop.
C I do not reject his observation as untrue, much less con-
  demn the person himself as a _liar_, whensoever it seems to
  be contradicted. @Boyle.
V Thy better soul abhors a _liar_’s part,
  Wise is thy voice, and noble is thy heart. @Pope’s Odyssey.
E {Li´ard.} _adj._
S 1. Mingled roan. @Markham.
S 2. _Liard_ in Scotland denotes gray-haired: as, he’s a _liard_ old
  man.
E {Liba´tion.} _n. s._ [_libatio_, Latin.]
S 1. The act of pouring wine on the ground in honour of some
  deity.
C In digging new earth pour in some wine, that the vapour
  of the earth and wine may comfort the spirits, provided it
  be not taken for a heathen sacrifice, or _libation_ to the earth.
  @Bacon’s Natural History.
S 2. The wine so poured.
C They had no other crime to object against the Christians,
  but that they did not offer up _libations_, and the smoke of
  sacrifices, to dead men. @Stillingfleet on Rom. Idolatry.
V The goblet then she took, with nectar crown’d,
  Sprinkling the first _libations_ on the ground. @Dryden’s Æn.
