E {Li´bbard.} _n. s._ [_liebard_, German; _leopardus_, Lat.] A leopard.
V Make the _libbard_ stern,
  Leave roaring, when in rage he for revenge did yearn.
  @Spenser’s Fairy Queen, b. {i.}
V The _libbard_, and the tiger, as the mole
  Rising, the crumbled earth above them threw. @Milton.
C The torrid parts of Africk are by Piso resembled to a
  _libbard_’s skin, the distance of whose spots represent the dis-
  persness of habitations, or towns of Africk. @Brerewood.
E {LI´BEL.} _n. s._ [_libellus_, Latin; _libelle_, French.]
S 1. A satire; defamatory writing; a lampoon.
C Are we reproached for the name of Christ? that ignominy
  serves but to advance our future glory; every such _libel_ here
  becomes panegyrick there. @Decay of Piety.
V Good heav’n! that sots and knaves should be so vain,
  To wish their vile resemblance may remain!
  And stand recorded, at their own request,
  To future days, a _libel_ or a jest. @Dryden.
S 2. [In the civil law.] A declaration or charge in writing against
  a person in court.
E To {Li´bel.} _v. n._ [from the noun.] To spread defamation;
  generally written or printed.
V Sweet scrauls to fly about the streets of Rome:
  What’s this but _libelling_ against the senate? @Shakespeare.
V He, like a privileg’d spy, whom nothing can
  Discredit, _libels_ now ’gainst each great man. @Donne.
E To {Li´bel.} _v. a._ To satirise; to lampoon;
C Is then the peerage of England any thing dishonoured
  when a peer suffers for his treason? if he be _libelled_, or any
  way defamed, he has his scandalum magnatum to punish the
  offender. @Dryden.
V But what so pure which envious tongues will spare?
  Some wicked wits have _libelled_ all the fair. @Pope.
E {Li´beller.} _n. s._ [from _libel_.] A defamer by writing; a lam-
  pooner.
C Our common _libellers_ are as free from the imputation of
  wit, as of morality. @Dryden’s Juvenal.
C The squibs are those who, in the common phrase, are
  called _libellers_ and lampooners. @Tatler.
C The common _libellers_, in their invectives, tax the church
  with an insatiable desire of power and wealth, equally com-
  mon to all bodies of men. @Swift.
E {Li´bellous.} _n. s._ [from _libel_.] Defamatory.
C It was the most malicious surmise that had ever been brew-
  ed, howsoever countenanced by a _libellous_ pamphlet. @Wotton.
E {Li´beral.} _adj._ [_liberalis_, Latin; _liberal_. French.]
S 1. Not mean; not low in birth; not low in mind.
S 2. Becoming a gentleman.
S 3. Munificent; generous; bountiful; not parcimonious.
V Her name was Mercy, well known over all
  To be both gracious and eke _liberal_. @Spens. Fa. Queen.
V Sparing would shew a worse sin than ill doctrine.
  Men of his way should be most _liberal_,
  They’re set here for examples. @Shakesp. Henry {VIII.}
V Needs must the pow’r
  That made us, and for us this ample world,
  Be infinitely good, and of his good
  As _liberal_ and free, as infinite. @Milton.
C There is no art better than to be _liberal_ of praise and
  commendation to others, in that wherein a man’s self hath
  any perfection. @Bacon’s Essays.
V The _liberal_ are secure alone;
  For what we frankly give, for ever is our own. @Granville.
C Several clergymen, otherwise little fond of obscure terms,
  are, in their sermons, very _liberal_ of all those which they
  find in ecclesiastical writers, as if it were our duty to under-
  stand them. @Swift.
E {Libera´lity.} _n. s._ [_liberalitas_, Latin; _liberalité_, Fr.] Mu-
  nificence; bounty; generosity; generous profusion.
V Why should he despair, that knows to court
  With words, fair looks, and _liberality?_ @Shakespeare.
V Such moderation with thy bounty join,
  That thou may’st nothing give that is not thine;
  That _liberality_ is but cast away,
  Which makes us borrow what we cannot pay. @Denham.
E {Libera´lly.} _adv._ [from _liberal_.] Bounteously; bountifully;
  largely.
C If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that
  giveth to all men _liberally_, and upbraideth not. @James {i. 5.}
E {Li´bertine.} _n. s._ [_libertin_, French.]
S 1. One unconfined; one at liberty.
V When he speaks,
  The air, a charter’d _libertine_, is still;
  And the mute wonder lurketh in men’s ears,
  To steal his sweet and honied sentences. @Shakesp. Hen. {V.}
S 2. One who lives without restraint or law.
V Man, the lawless _libertine_, may rove
  Free and unquestion’d. @Rowe’s Jane Shore.
C Want of power is the only bound that a _libertine_ puts to
  his views upon any of the sex. @Clarissa.
