S 2. One who pays no regard to the precepts of religion.
V They say this town is full of couzenage,
  As nimble jugglers, that deceive the eye;
  Disguised cheaters, prating mountebanks,
  And many such like _libertines_ of sin. @Shakespeare.
C That word may be applied to some few _libertines_ in the
  audience. @Collier’s View of the Stage.
S 3. [In law; _libertinus_, Lat.] A freedman; or rather, the son
  of a freedman.
C Some persons are forbidden to be accusers on the score of
  their sex, as women; others on the score of their age, as
  pupils and infants; others on the score of their conditions, as
  _libertines_ against their patrons. @Ayliffe’s Parergon.
E {Li´bertine.} _adj._ [_libertin_, French.] Licentious; irreligious.
C There are men that marry not, but chuse rather a _libertine_
  and impure single life, than to be yoked in marriage. @Bacon.
C Might not the queen make diligent enquiry, if any person
  about her should happen to be of _libertine_ principles or mo-
  rals. @Swift’s Project for Advancement of Religion.
E {Li´bertinism.} _n. s._ [from _libertine_.] Irreligion; licentiousness
  of opinions and practice.
C That spirit of religion and seriousness vanished all at once,
  and a spirit of liberty and _libertinism_, of infidelity and pro-
  faneness, started up in the room of it. @Atterbury’s Sermons.
E {Li´berty.} _n. s._ [_liberté_, French; _libertas_, Latin.]
S 1. Freedom, as opposed to slavery.
C My master knows of your being here, and hath threatened
  to put me into everlasting _liberty_, if I tell you of it; for he
  swears, he’ll turn me away. @Shakespeare.
V O _liberty!_ thou goddess, heav’nly bright!
  Profuse of bliss, and pregnant with delight,
  Eternal pleasures in thy presence reign. @Addison.
S 2. Freedom, as opposed to necessity.
C _Liberty_ is the power in any agent to do, or forbear, any
  particular action, according to the determination, or thought
  of the mind, whereby either of them is preferred to the
  other. @Locke.
C As it is in the motions of the body, so it is in the thoughts
  of our minds: where any one is such, that we have power
  to take it up, or lay it by, according to the preference of the
  mind, there we are at _liberty_. @Locke.
S 2. Privilege; exemption; immunity.
C His majesty gave not an intire country to any, much less
  did he grant jura regalia, or any extraordinary _liberties_. @Davies.
S 4. Relaxation of restraint.
S 5. Leave; permission.
C I shall take the _liberty_ to consider a third ground, which,
  with some men, has the same authority. @Locke.
E {Libi´dinous.} _n. s._ [_libidinosus_, Latin.] Lewd; lustful.
C None revolt from the faith; because they must not look upon
  a woman to lust after her, but because they are much more
  restrained from the perpetration of their lusts. If wanton
  glances and _libidinous_ thoughts had been permitted by the gos-
  pel, they would have apostatized nevertheless. @Bentley.
E {Libi´dinously.} _adv._ [from _libidinous_.] Lewdly; lustfully.
E {Li´bral.} _adj._ [_libralis_, Latin.] Of a pound weight. @Dict.
E {Libra´rian.} _n. s._ [_librarius_, Latin.]
S 1. One who has the care of a library.
S 2. One who transcribes or copies books.
C Charybdis thrice swallows, and thrice refunds, the waves:
  this must be understood of regular tides. There are indeed
  but two tides in a day, but this is the error of the _libra-
  rians_. @Broome’s Notes on the Odyssey.
E {Li´brary.} _n. s._ [_librarie_, Fr.] A large collection of books,
  publick or private.
V Then as they ’gan his _library_ to view,
  And antique registers for to avise,
  There chanced to the prince’s hand to rise
  An ancient book, hight Briton’s monuments. @Fa. Qu.
V Make choice of all my _library_,
  And so beguile thy sorrow. @Shakesp. Titus Andronicus.
C I have given you the _library_ of a painter, and a catalogue
  of such books as he ought to read. @Dryden’s Dufresnoy.
E To {Li´brate.} _v. a._ [_libro_, Latin.] To poise; to balance;
  to hold in equipoise.
E {Libra´tion.} _n. s._ [_libratio_, Latin; _libration_, French.]
S 1. The state of being balanced.
C This is what may be said of the balance, and the _libra-
  tion_, of the body. @Dryden’s Dufresnoy.
V Their pinions still
  In loose _librations_ stretch’d, to trust the void
  Trembling refuse. @Thomson’s Spring.
S 2. [In astronomy.]
C _Libration_ is the balancing motion or trepidation in the fir-
  mament, whereby the declination of the sun, and the lati-
  tude of the stars, change from time to time. Astronomers
  likewise ascribe to the moon a _libratory_ motion, or motion of
  trepidation, which they pretend is from east to west, and
  from north to south, because that, at full moon, they some-
  times discover parts of her disk which are not discovered at
@folio 5
@column
  other times. These kinds are called, the one a _libration_
  in longitude, and the other a _libration_ in latitude. Besides
  this, there is a third kind, which they call an apparent _libra-
  tion_, and which consists in this, that when the moon is at
  her greatest elongation from the south, her axis being then
  almost perpendicular to the plane of the ecliptick, the sun
  must enlighten towards the north pole of the moon some
  parts which he did not before, and that, on the contrary,
  some parts of those which he enlightened towards the oppo-
  site pole are obscured; and this produces the same effect which
  the _libration_ in latitude does. @Dict. Trev.
C Those planets which move upon their axis, do not all
  make intire revolutions; for the moon maketh only a kind
  of _libration_, or a reciprocated motion on her own axis. @Grew.
E {Li´bratory.} _adj._ [from _libro_, Lat.] Balancing; playing like
  a balance.
E {Lice}, the plural of _louse_.
V Red blisters rising on their paps appear,
  And flaming carbuncles, and noisome sweat,
  And clammy dews, that loathsome _lice_ beget;
  Till the slow creeping evil eats his way. @Dryden’s Virg.
E {Li´cebane.} _n. s._ [_lice_ and _bane_.] A plant.
E {LI´CENSE.} _n. s._ [_licentia_, Latin; _licence_, French.]
S 1. Exorbitant liberty; contempt of legal and necessary restraint.
C Some of the wiser seeing that a popular _licence_ is indeed the
  many-headed tyranny, prevailed with the rest to make Mu-
  sidorus their chief. @Sidney.
V Taunt my faults
  With such full _licence_, as both truth and malice
  Have power to utter. @Shakesp. Ant. and Cleopatra.
V They baul for freedom in their senseless moods,
  And still revolt when truth would set them free;
  _Licence_ they mean, when they cry liberty. @Milton.
V The privilege that ancient poets claim,
  Now turn’d to _license_ by too just a name. @Roscommon.
C Though this be a state of liberty, yet it is not a state of
  _licence_; though man, in that state, have an uncontroulable
  liberty to dispose of his person or possessions, yet he has not
  liberty to destroy himself. @Locke.
S 2. A grant of permission.
C They sent some to bring them a _licence_ from the senate.
  @Judith {xi. 14.}
C Those few abstract names that the schools forged, and put
  into the mouths of their scholars, could never yet get admit-
  tance into common use, or obtain the _licence_ of publick ap-
  probation. @Locke.
C We procured a _licence_ of the duke of Parma to enter the
  theatre and gallery. @Addison on Italy.
S 3. Liberty; permission.
C It is not the manner of the Romans to deliver any man
  to die, before that he which is accused have the accusers
  face to face, and have _licence_ to answer for himself. @Acts.
E To {Li´cense.} _v. a._ [_licencier_, French.]
S 1. To set at liberty.
C He would play well, and willingly, at some games of
  greatest attention, which shewed, that when he listed he
  could _license_ his thoughts. @Wotton.
S 2. To permit by a legal grant.
V Wit’s titans brav’d the skies,
  And the press groan’d with _licens’d_ blasphemies. @Pope.
E {Li´censer.} _n. s._ [from _license_.] A granter of permission; com-
  monly a tool of power.
E {Lice´ntiate.} _n. s._ [_licentiatus_, low Latin.]
S 1. A man who uses license.
C The _licentiates_ somewhat licentiously, least they should
  prejudice poetical liberty, will pardon themselves for doubling
  or rejecting a letter, if the sense fall aptly. @Camden.
S 2. A degree in Spanish universities.
C A man might, after that time, sue for the degree of a _li-
  centiate_ or master in this faculty. @Ayliffe’s Parergon.
E To {Lice´ntiate.} _v. a._ [_licentier_, French.] To permit; to
  encourage by license.
C We may not hazard either the stifling of generous inclina-
  tions, or the _licentiating_ of any thing that is coarse. @L’Estrange.
E {Lice´ntious.} _n. s._ [_licencieux_, French; _licentiosus_, Latin.]
S 1. Unrestrained by law or morality.
V Later ages pride, like corn-fed steed,
  Abus’d her plenty, and fat swoln encrease,
  To all _licentious_ lust, and gan exceed
  The measure of her mean, and natural first need. @Fa. Qu.
V How would it touch thee to the quick,
  Should’st thou but hear I were _licentious?_
  And that this body, consecrate to thee,
  With ruffian lust should be contaminate. @Shakespeare.
S 2. Presumptuous; unconfined.
V The Tyber, whose _licentious_ waves,
  So often overflow’d the neighbouring fields,
  Now runs a smooth and inoffensive course. @Roscommon.
E {Lice´ntiously.} _adv._ [from _licentious_.] With too much liber-
  ty; without just restraint.
